We've been under the redidender.
我们一直在这个红……

00:00:59.986 --> 00:01:01.140
It's the redidender.
就是这个红……

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:02.906
We had so much to catch up on.
我们有太多事情要聊了。

00:01:02.906 --> 00:01:05.379
We haven't met the computers since Monday.
我们从周一开始就没见过电脑了。

00:01:05.379 --> 00:01:06.168
All right.
好吧。

00:01:06.168 --> 00:01:08.140
So, Alec, what do we got?
那么，亚历克，我们有什么？

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:08.730
Here we go.
我们开始吧。

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:18.290
So one of our one of our biggest supporters, not our biggest or the show's biggest supporters Swarly memes has a lot to say and So Swarly means I'm sure you're listening to this.
我们的一位，或者说我们最大的，或者说剧集最大的粉丝之一——Swarly memes有很多话要说，所以Swarly memes，我确信你在听这个。

00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:36.980
I hope that currently this is being clipped like right now I'm in a Swarly meme that would be incredibly exciting and Swarly meme says Chris Harris That's the mini cherry on top of the regular cherry on top of the Sunday of awesomeness that is this podcast and then And I'm just going to read this lovely note to Chris with the question at the end.
我希望现在这期节目正在被剪辑成短视频，此刻我正处于一个Swarly meme里，那将是难以置信的兴奋，Swarly memes说：“克里斯·哈里斯，这简直是这场精彩绝伦的播客圣代冰淇淋上锦上添花的那个樱桃，再加一层又加一层的樱桃。”然后，我只想给克里斯读一下这条可爱的留言，最后还有一个问题。

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:53.780
Chris, what I love about your episodes is how we always find ourselves in silly situations like game night, club night, award night, concert night, removing tattoo, fixing a car, hippie wedding venue, effed up, house or subway, but they still have so much heart and force the characters to reveal their true selves or grow into a better person.
克里斯，我最喜欢你的剧集的地方在于，我们总会发现自己陷入一些疯狂的情境中，比如游戏之夜、俱乐部之夜、颁奖之夜、演唱会之夜、洗纹身、修车、嬉皮士婚礼场地、糟糕的房子或地铁，但这些剧集仍然充满了温情，迫使角色展现出真实的自我，或者成长为更好的人。

00:01:53.780 --> 00:02:02.469
Because in the end, it's not those silly moments with the people we love dearly that we remember, not the big weddings and whatnot.
因为到头来，我们记住的并不是和我们深爱的人在一起时那些疯狂的时刻，不是盛大的婚礼之类的事情。

00:02:02.469 --> 00:02:15.430
So to turn that into a bit of a question, Chris, can you just because you do these larger episodes often going all the way up to subway wars and that size, can you start putting together the scope versus the character?
所以把这个转化为一个问题，克里斯，你能不能——因为你经常创作这些宏大的剧集，一直到《地铁大战》这种规模的——你能谈谈场面规模与人物塑造的关系吗？

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:30.189
Yeah, I'm torn between such a lovely compliment and also like being exposed for having a very simple formula for everything I've ever written.
是的，我一方面觉得这是个非常可爱的赞美，另一方面又觉得自己被曝光了，原来我写过的所有东西都有一个非常简单的公式。

00:02:30.189 --> 00:02:31.189
I don't know.
我不知道。

00:02:31.189 --> 00:02:41.310
I don't know if I think of it as, you know, it's always, you know, how much a mother was always great about these are the big moments in this person's life.
我不知道我是否会这样想，你知道的，《老爸老妈浪漫史》一直很棒的一点就是，这些是一个人生活中的重大时刻。

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:52.949
And that's the virtue of the perspective of the dad talking to his kids years later is like, we have an excuse to skip over the small things and talk about like, this is when I started dating this person.
这也是父亲多年后对孩子们说话这种视角的好处，就好像我们有一个借口可以跳过琐事，直接谈论诸如“这是我开始和这个人约会的时候”、“这是这两人分手的时候”、“这是这个人回归的时候”。

00:02:52.949 --> 00:02:53.949
This is when these two broke up.
这就是这两人分手的时候。

00:02:53.949 --> 00:02:56.590
This is when this person returned.
这是这个人回归的时候。

00:02:56.590 --> 00:03:10.629
And so I think the big events go along with that, and they're always, they're always fun to put people in, to put characters in a new situation, obviously, just to see how they'll react.
所以我觉得重大事件与之相辅相成，把人们、把角色置于新的情境中总是很有趣，显然，只是为了看他们会如何反应。

00:03:10.629 --> 00:03:17.469
But no, I feel lucky that I got to write a lot of these, especially in these early seasons.
不过说真的，我觉得自己很幸运能写出这么多集，尤其是在前几季。

00:03:17.469 --> 00:03:19.909
You know, Carter and Craig came in with the idea about the club.
你知道的，卡特和克雷格带着关于俱乐部的想法来了。

00:03:19.909 --> 00:03:23.270
They came in with Barney and Game Night.
他们带着巴尼和游戏之夜来了。

00:03:23.270 --> 00:03:31.789
So it was a lot of fun to play with those worlds that the characters got to jump into.
所以能玩转那些角色们纵身跃入的世界，真的非常有趣。

00:03:31.789 --> 00:03:40.349
It's also like there are big events and how I met your mother, but you could make the argument that every episode is a big event or he wouldn't be telling the story.
这就好比《老爸老妈浪漫史》里有很多重大事件，但你也可以争辩说，每一集都是重大事件，否则他就不会讲这个故事了。

00:03:40.349 --> 00:03:41.349
Yeah.
对。

00:03:41.349 --> 00:03:47.990
It feels like if he's telling it, it lingered for him and it also shaped him.
感觉既然他在讲，说明这件事对他来说挥之不去，并且也塑造了他。

00:03:47.990 --> 00:03:56.969
When we pitched the show, we pitched like it's the 100 best stories of your 20s and 30s, hoping that we'd hit 100 episodes and then we did 208.
当我们推销这部剧时，我们说这是你20多岁和30多岁时最棒的100个故事，当时希望我们能拍到100集，结果我们拍了208集。

00:03:56.969 --> 00:04:01.889
So that back 100 Nate, we're just more like B minus, C plus.
所以后来的那100集，内特，我们更像是B减、C加水平。

00:04:01.889 --> 00:04:06.030
But it's also like the, is it Gene Shepherd who you keep referring to the critical story?
但这也像是，是吉恩·谢泼德吗？你一直提到那个关键故事？

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:07.030
Yeah, Gene Shepherd.
对，吉恩·谢泼德。

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:08.030
Yeah.
对。

00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:09.030
Yeah.
对。

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:10.030
It's the pineapple incident.
这就是菠萝事件。

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:17.350
We talk a lot about like Ted is mythologizing his life, you know, he's like, we all do, right?
我们经常讨论泰德在将他的人生神话化，你知道的，他就像我们所有人一样，对吧？

00:04:17.350 --> 00:04:23.483
We're all the main characters in our stories and what are the things you'll remember 25 years from 2005?
我们都是自己故事中的主角，在2005年之后的25年里，你会记住哪些事情？

00:04:23.483 --> 00:04:26.313
And that was the test of season one of the show.
那是该剧第一季的考验。

00:04:26.313 --> 00:04:30.028
And I think Game Night was a good one because it's so eventful.
我觉得《游戏之夜》是个很好的例子，因为它发生了太多事。

00:04:30.028 --> 00:04:37.990
It raises the bar on how eventful and memorable you have to try to make things in the show because he has to remember it in the future.
它提高了你在剧中必须努力让事情变得多么富有事件性和难忘的标准，因为他必须在未来记住它。

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:47.430
And I love that notion, Josh, like Ted's mythologizing his own because I do feel like that's such a part of it is, you know, even the re-returned, like giving these names to these events.
乔希，我很喜欢这个概念，比如泰德将自己的经历神话化，因为我觉得这确实是其中的一部分，你知道的，甚至连“再次回归”也是如此，给这些事件起这样的名字。

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:48.828
The re-returned, yes.
再次回归，是的。

00:04:48.828 --> 00:04:51.359
Very Jean Shepherd, making the events.
非常吉恩·谢泼德风格，让事件变成现实。

00:04:51.359 --> 00:04:53.624
It's a historical document, right?
这是一份历史文献，对吧？

00:04:53.624 --> 00:04:56.088
It's like the Battle of Valley Forge.
就像福吉谷战役一样。

00:04:56.088 --> 00:05:00.284
And also the kind of coded language that friend groups develop.
还有朋友圈里形成的某种暗语。

00:05:00.284 --> 00:05:02.882
You know, you have this kind of Patois.
你知道，你们有这种特殊的方言。

00:05:02.882 --> 00:05:15.670
Craig, Sarah had this question a few weeks ago and it fits nicely now of like, you know, the Supreme Court, if the chief justices is in the majority, they get to choose who writes the opinion.
克雷格，莎拉几周前问过这个问题，现在把它放进来正合适：你知道的，最高法院里，如果首席大法官在多数派中，他们就可以选择谁来写意见书。

00:05:15.670 --> 00:05:22.687
You and Carter obviously got to choose who writes each episode was Chris at a certain point chosen for a certain scope, a certain kind of episode.
你和卡特显然可以挑选谁来写每一集，克里斯在某个特定时刻是被选中去写某种特定规模、某种特定类型的剧集吗？

00:05:22.687 --> 00:05:27.398
And in general, how did you decide how many you guys would write and how many you would give away?
总的来说，你们是怎么决定你们俩写多少集、分给别人多少集的？

00:05:27.398 --> 00:05:33.695
Boy, I mean, in season one, I can't say there was much of a formula that was more just like keeping our head above water and stuff.
天哪，我是说，在第一季中，我不能说有什么固定的公式，那更多只是像竭尽全力维持生计之类的。

00:05:33.695 --> 00:05:37.300
And I think, you know, Chris, I think you were three of them in season one.
而且我认为，你知道，克里斯，我觉得你在第一季里写了三集。

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:37.973
Is that right?
是这样吗？

00:05:37.973 --> 00:05:38.550
I did, yeah.
我写了，是的。

00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:41.829
And I'd say that that's because we knew Chris would kill it.
我要说的是，那是因为我们知道克里斯会大获成功。

00:05:45.750 --> 00:05:53.395
You're excited to give a guy like Chris an episode because you know it's going to come in really good and you're not not need to have late nights on that one.
把一集交给像克里斯这样的人你会感到兴奋，因为你知道这一集效果会非常好，而且你不需要为那一集熬夜。

00:05:53.395 --> 00:05:55.766
At that point, we had never run a TV show before.
当时，我们从来没有运作过电视剧。

00:05:55.766 --> 00:05:59.395
So at that point, I can't say it was anything more than this was a big one.
所以在那个时候，我不能说这不仅仅是因为这是一个大制作。

00:05:59.395 --> 00:06:00.847
We're learning who Barney was.
我们在了解巴尼是谁。

00:06:00.847 --> 00:06:05.056
It had to advance the story in the present while showing us who Barney was in the past.
它必须在展示巴尼过去的同​​时，推进现在的剧情。

00:06:05.056 --> 00:06:07.186
High degree of difficulty, give it to Chris.
难度系数极高，交给克里斯。

00:06:07.186 --> 00:06:10.670
I think that was part of the, so it was a compliment to Chris, for sure.
我想这是其中的一部分原因，所以这绝对是对克里斯的赞美。

00:06:10.670 --> 00:06:23.550
But it also felt to me like in the arc of who was writing what, it always felt like the first episode was a you and Carter, the finales were you and Carter, and one or two in the middle were you and Carter, right?
但对我来说，在谁写什么的脉络中，感觉总是第一季是你们和卡特，大结局是你们和卡特，中间有一两集是你们和卡特，对吧？

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:39.750
Yeah, more than one or two, we usually did, because there were two of us, and we could, one guy could still be running the day to day of the show, and the other guy could peel off and write one, and it was sort of like, that's how we do it, one of us would sort of mostly do one episode and then the other, but both our names were on it.
对，不止一两集，我们通常都会参与，因为我们有两个人，我们可以一个人继续负责剧集的日常运作，另一个人抽身出来写一集，这有点像是我们这样做的，我们中的一个人通常会主要负责一集，然后是另一个人，但我们两个人的名字都会写在上面。

00:06:39.750 --> 00:06:42.189
Yeah, it's really like a Lennon McCartney situation, right?
对，这真的很像列侬和麦卡特尼的情况，对吧？

00:06:42.189 --> 00:06:46.389
Like McCartney wrote Blackbird, but it's a Lennon McCartney song.
就像麦卡特尼写了《Blackbird》，但它是一首列侬-麦卡特尼的歌。

00:06:46.389 --> 00:06:49.230
Yeah, except like a much worse Lennon and McCartney.
对，除了像个糟糕得多的列侬和麦卡特尼。

00:06:49.230 --> 00:06:54.629
Like them, but nowhere near as talented.
就像他们一样，但远没有那么有才华。

00:06:54.629 --> 00:06:56.389
So that kind of gives you the scope.
所以这大致让你了解了规模。

00:06:56.389 --> 00:06:58.870
And everyone's all, I come in with my octopus's garden.
每个人都在说，我带着我的《章鱼的花园》来了。

00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:01.790
And it's great to have you here, Ringo.
很高兴你能来，林戈。

00:07:01.790 --> 00:07:02.629
Oh my God.
哦，天哪。

00:07:02.629 --> 00:07:06.189
Here comes the sun, motherfucker.
太阳出来了，他妈的。

00:07:06.189 --> 00:07:07.550
Here comes the sun.
太阳出来了。

00:07:07.550 --> 00:07:08.550
That's Chris Harris.
那是克里斯·哈里斯。

00:07:08.550 --> 00:07:09.550
Come on.
拜托。

00:07:09.550 --> 00:07:10.550
Don't.
别这样。

00:07:10.550 --> 00:07:13.629
But by the way, I hope this is Garden's fucking great, too.
但顺便说一句，我希望《章鱼的花园》也他妈的很棒。

00:07:13.629 --> 00:07:15.629
No one's losing out here.
这里没有人是输家。

00:07:15.629 --> 00:07:16.629
We love you, Ringo.
我们爱你，林戈。

00:07:16.629 --> 00:07:17.629
But no.
不过说真的。

00:07:17.629 --> 00:07:28.790
But yeah, Carter and I, yeah, we would sort of mostly take one or the other, but it would always kind of go through the other guy first and say, hey, add some awesomeness, or sometimes help dig me out of a problem on this.
但卡特和我，是的，我们基本上会各自负责一集，但通常总是先让对方过目，然后说：“嘿，加点精彩元素，或者有时帮我摆脱这个困境。”

00:07:28.790 --> 00:07:31.550
So we would each weigh in on each other's episodes.
所以我们都会对彼此的剧集提出意见。

00:07:31.550 --> 00:07:35.189
But over time it became more like Carter ran a room, I ran a room and then we sort of tripped.
但随着时间的推移，它变得更像是卡特管理一个编剧室，我管理一个编剧室，然后我们有点分道扬镳了。

00:07:35.189 --> 00:07:36.189
So it was like that.
所以它就像那样。

00:07:36.189 --> 00:07:41.790
But Chris, we would have given Chris this episode with Great Glee knowing it was a really important big one.
不过克里斯，我们本来就会很高兴把这集交给克里斯，因为知道这真的是非常重要的一大集。

00:07:41.790 --> 00:07:42.790
Okay.
好的。

00:07:42.790 --> 00:07:56.209
So a lot of questions, Chris, from the fans, we were talking about the fact that fans, especially ones who came to it later or binging it, they get the whole series as a book that they just read all at once.
所以有很多关于粉丝的问题，克里斯，我们一直在讨论这样一个事实：粉丝们，尤其是那些后来才看或者一口气刷完的人，他们把整部剧当作一本书，一下子就读完了。

00:07:56.209 --> 00:07:59.370
Whereas you're making it as a newspaper, right?
而你们制作它就像是在办一份报纸，对吧？

00:07:59.370 --> 00:08:12.286
to sort of make you one every day and hope that if you see a fire truck in one episode and seven years later there's another fire truck where you're planning on there being two fire trucks you right they kind of see all of that.
每天为你炮制一份，希望如果他们在某一集里看到一辆消防车，而在七年后的另一集里又看到另一辆消防车，而你计划中本来就有两辆消防车，他们就会把这些全都串联起来。

00:08:12.286 --> 00:08:14.608
So there's a couple of questions on that.
所以关于这一点有几个问题。

